confirmed.description:
The fork added INDEX_op_rint_f32/f64 to the i386 backend implemented with x87 FRNDINT:

    OP_f32_f64(rint):
        /* FRNDINT uses the host x87 CW rounding mode (not MXCSR). ... */
        tcg_out_fld_xmm(s, a1, dp);
        tcg_out_frndint(s);
        tcg_out_fstp_xmm(s, a0, dp);

Because the host x87 control word is never programmed (gen_flcr writes only MXCSR: `tcg_out_st(s, TCG_TYPE_I32, a0, TCG_REG_ESP, -8); LDMXCSR` at tcg-target.c.inc:3109-3112), this rounds to nearest unconditionally. The 2026-08-09 fixes correctly worked around that by routing the affected guest ops to softfloat helpers on non-AArch64 hosts (target/i386/tcg/translate.c:2207-2220 for FRNDINT, :2130-2143/:2154 for FIST RC 1/2). So on the target Haswell/Windows build every guest FRNDINT under a non-nearest control word — i.e. the standard MSVCRT floor()/ceil() sequence (fnstcw / or RC / fldcw / frndint / fldcw) — takes a full floatx80 softfloat helper call plus the gen_flush_fp register spill, and every FIST under RC=1/2 does the same. RC=0 FIST stays inline but pays MOVQ [rsp-0x10] -> FLD m64 -> FRNDINT (microcoded, ~20+ cycles on Haswell) -> FSTP m64 -> MOVQ, i.e. two store-forwarding round trips through the SysV red zone plus a microcoded x87 op, where the mode-correct answer is one SSE instruction.

SSE4.1 ROUNDSS/ROUNDSD (0x0A/0x0B with P_EXT3A|P_DATA16) with imm8=0x04 means 'round to integer using the current MXCSR RC' — which is exactly what gen_flcr already programmed to the guest's RC (translate.c:1797-1811 writes (rc<<13)|0x1f80, and the x87 RC encoding 0/1/2/3 = nearest/down/up/trunc is bit-identical to the MXCSR RC encoding). One VROUNDSD makes rint_f32/f64 correct for ALL four guest rounding modes on x86_64, removes the x87 stack use, removes the [rsp-0x10] red-zone traffic (which the Windows x64 ABI does not reserve — legal on SysV, merely conventional-by-luck on Windows), and lets both `#if !defined(__aarch64__)` softfloat fallbacks in translate.c be deleted.

The i386 backend has no SSE4.1 cpuinfo bit today (host/include/i386/host/cpuinfo.h defines CPUINFO_SSE2 at bit 7 and CPUINFO_AVX1 at bit 9 — bit 8 is free), but `have_avx1` is already available and AVX1 strictly implies SSE4.1, so gating on have_avx1 (true on the target i7-4770K) is a zero-risk conservative start with the existing x87 path kept as the pre-AVX fallback.

