plausible.description:
On Windows, when xemu is launched without a parent console (the normal double-click case), main() redirects stderr to a file: freopen("xemu.log", "a", stderr) (ui/xemu.c:1946). A stream returned by freopen onto a disk file is fully buffered by default, and nothing in the tree calls setvbuf on it afterwards. Every diagnostic in this slice writes with a bare fprintf(stderr, ...) and never flushes:

  - the XEMU_PFIFO_HEARTBEAT print (pfifo.c:572), whose entire reason for existing is triaging a hang - precisely the case where the last buffered lines are the ones you need and the process never gets to flush them;
  - the XEMU_NV2A_NSPROF 5 s summary (nsprof.c:167-179);
  - the atexit-registered censuses (pfifo.c:492 idle-park, pgraph.c:223 pfifo_kick, pgraph.c:263 PGRAPH MMIO), which additionally never run at all if the process is killed or terminates via _exit.

On macOS stderr goes to a terminal or the unified log and is line-buffered, so the problem is invisible there; this is a Windows-only diagnostic gap that will silently waste the first real Windows triage session.

